  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 AM MDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nThe dominant western ridging pattern holds firm again today, the\nthe H5 ridge still anchored on the Four Corners region. The\nsouthwest tap of monsoon moisture continues today, and most\nmodels are bringing an increase in moisture during the next 6 to\n12 hours. With warm temperatures still in place, daytime showers\nand thunderstorms will again develop late this morning and\ncontinue into the afternoon, moving to the northeast. The\nincreased moisture and shortwave energy passing through today \nwill bring a better chance for measurable rainfall from any \nthunderstorms, but the good forward motion of any storms will \nkeep the chance for flooding low. Current cloud cover and any \ncover from thunderstorms today will keep temperatures lower \nthan Sunday. Highs will still be above normal, but will hold in \nthe low to mid 90s through the afternoon.\n\nTuesday will be similar to today, with very warm temperatures\nduring the day. Ample moisture will tap this heat for scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms during the afternoon. Midlevel support\nmay be more limited, so showers might end up more isolated.\n\nWednesday another more vigorous shortwave comes around the ridge\nthrough Wyoming, which is expected to bring a much better chance\nfor showers and thunderstorms. After this feature passes\nthrough, models are shifting the ridge slightly to the west\nthrough the rest of the week into the upcoming weekend. Models\nare noting a significant drop in moisture, as the monsoon\nmoisture gets cut off. This would lead to much drier and warmer\nperiod heading into the weekend.",
